"Prediction markets give Lynne Walz only about a 5-10% win probability", "Pillen negatives (undocumented-labor report, Farm Bureau neutrality) and independents on the ballot add uncertainty"], "rationale": "Pillen's incumbency in a state leaning Republican by ten points makes him a clear favorite, but the strongest recent surveys put his lead in the mid-to-high single digits and markets price Walz's chances near one in ten, keeping this at Likely R rather than Safe R. Scattered Pillen controversies and third-party candidates leave room for the margin to move but not the favorite.", "watch": "A late nonpartisan poll or further fallout from the report on Pillen's workforce could push the margin toward a low-single-digit race or back toward double digits.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jim Pillen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's governor's race pits Republican incumbent Jim Pillen against Democrat Lynne Walz, with independents also on the ballot. The state's Republican lean and Pillen's incumbency make him the clear favorite, and most race ratings call the contest solid or likely Republican.\n\nPolling has run thinner and closer than those ratings suggest. September surveys from across the spectrum put Pillen's lead between one and seventeen points, with the most recent nonpartisan-style polls showing mid-to-high single digits, and a Democratic-sponsored poll in late September showed a tie. Prediction markets give Walz roughly a one-in-ten chance. Pillen has drawn some unflattering coverage, including a report about undocumented workers at his operation and the Farm Bureau's decision to stay neutral, while Walz has courted Republican endorsements. The seat appears likely to stay in Republican hands, with the size of the margin the main open question.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date